    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §364-j, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2127\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to school-based\nhealth centers for purposes of managed care programs under medicaid\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo permit each school-based health center (SBHC) to choose remain out of\nMedicaid managed care and to guide cooperation between SBHCs and Medi-\ncaid managed care providers.\n\n \nSUMMARY PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends Social Services Law § 364-J to define \"school based\nhealth center\" and a \"sponsoring organization\" as providing an array of\nprimary health care services in elementary, secondary and pre kindergar-\nten public school settings which are sponsored by general hospitals or\nhealth centers licensed under public health law Article 28.\n\nThe bill amends Social Services Law § 364-J to(A) provide SBHCS the\nchoice of operating under Medicaid fee-for-service, (B) enables them to\nchoose to participate in Medicaid managed care and C) to ensure coordi-\nnated and quality care as well as participation in quality improvement\ninitiatives, there shall be a DOH drafted memorandum between the SBHC\nand the Medicaid managed care provider, providing for data sharing to\nwhich the DOH will contribute.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSBHCs provide comprehensive physical, chronic, mental and dental health\nservices to over 230,000 underserved youth in rural, urban and suburban\nareas of the State, regardless of insurance status or ability to pay.\nSBHCs are a proven model for increasing access to health and mental\nhealth services, reducing ethnic and racial disparities in the communi-\nties they serve, and improving school attendance and performance. They\nhave a proven track record of reducing health care costs including\nunnecessary hospitalizations and emergency room visits.\n\nSince 1985, SBHCs have been \"carved-out\" of the MMC program, being paid\nunder fee-for-service. However, the Department of Health is now planning\nto end the carve-out as of January 1, 2021. SBHCs will be required to\nnegotiate the terms and conditions of payment by managed care plans. A\nreport by the Children's Defense Fund found that this transition will\ncost SBHCs over sixteen million dollars in lost revenue. Already, SBHCs\nhave suffered over seven million dollars or nearly 30% in funding cuts\nsince 2008, while their patient population has grown.\n\nThis bill protects the choice of each SBHC to operate either carved-out\nfrom Medicaid managed care or participating in Medicaid managed care.\nWhere some of a carved-out SBHC's patients are Medicaid managed care\nenrollees, the bill provides for the SBHC and the Department to provide\nextensive information to the student's managed care plan about the care\nprovided to the student. This will help the managed care plan to provide\nquality care and effective care coordination even though part of the\nstudent's care is not provided by the managed care plan.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-2020: S7812B/A9894B Gottfried\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days after becoming law.\n",
